{"step_index":1954,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T13:44:29Z","input_tokens":2138,"cache_read_tokens":253877,"output_tokens":1042,"content":"To share infrastructure **organically** (where multi-tenancy is natural) while keeping identity and private memory strictly sovereign, here is the clean boundary:\n\n---\n\n### 1. What is CORE and Shared Organically (Zero Leakage, Maximum Efficiency)\n\nThese are stateless, read-only, or multi-tenant infrastructure components where sharing provides massive performance and cost advantages:\n\n| Shared Component | Why It Shares Organically | How It Stays Clean |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **The Embedding & LLM Inference Engines** | Running one local embedding model (or API connection) is $10\\times$ faster and cheaper than running 10 separate inference servers. | **Stateless workers**: The embedding worker takes text in, returns vectors out. It retains zero conversation memory. |\n| **The Vector Database (Qdrant Server)** | Running one Qdrant instance on `:6333` / `:6334`. | **Isolated Collections or Payload Scopes**: Each instance queries only its own collection (`memory_<id>`) or filtered payload. Qdrant never joins across collections. |\n| **The Message Broker (EMQX)** | EMQX is architected from day one for multi-tenant pub/sub with millions of topics. | **Topic Namespacing & ACLs**: `opdbus/<instance_id>/#`. Instance A has zero access to Instance B’s topics. |\n| **The Base OS Rootfs (Immutable Seed)** | Sharing the identical Linux userspace saves tens of gigabytes of disk and RAM. | **Read-Only Btrfs Seed**: Shared via read-only views; neither instance can alter or see the other's writes. |\n| **The OSCAL Schema & Tool Definitions** | The 74 plugins and their typed method contracts (`get_health`, `code_search`, etc.) are identical for everyone. | **Sealed Blob Catalog**: Static, immutable, shared reflection metadata. |\n\n---\n\n### 2. What Must NEVER Be Shared (Strictly Sovereign & Private)\n\nThese represent the **individual identity and state**—if any of these are shared, privacy and autonomy break:\n\n| Sovereign per Instance | Location | Why It Must Be Isolated |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **The Identity Sled & WireGuard Keypair** | `@agent_user` subvolume / `/etc/identity/` | The cryptographic keys (`Env5z...`) that sign transactions and authorize operations. |\n| **Cozo Graph & Conversation Relational Store** | Dedicated **ext4** disk slice (`p2` of the instance loop image) | Private chat turns, personal relationships, episodic memory, and user PII. |\n| **The Writable Rootfs Sprout** | Dedicated **Btrfs leaf** (`p1` of the instance loop image) | Contains any instance-specific packages, daemon configurations, or modified `/var` state. |\n| **The Snowball Hashchain (Audit Ledger)** | Instance-specific chain path | The cryptographic provenance of what *this specific agent* did, thought, and approved. |\n\n---\n\n### The Organic Interaction (\"The Bridge\")\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                 SHARED ORGANIC SUBSTRATE                    │\n│                                                             │\n│   [EMQX Broker]        [Qdrant Engine]     [Embedding Hub]  │\n│   (Topic ACLs)         (Collection Scopes) (Stateless)      │\n└──────────▲────────────────────▲────────────────────▲────────┘\n           │                    │                    │\n   Scoped Topics         Scoped Vectors        Pure Vectors\n           │                    │                    │\n┌──────────▼────────────────────▼────────────────────▼────────┐\n│             SOVEREIGN IDENTITY A (Instance 1)               │\n│  • WireGuard Key A     • Cozo Store A (on private ext4)     │\n│  • Btrfs Sprout Leaf A • Snowball Chain A                   │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│             SOVEREIGN IDENTITY B (Instance 2)               │\n│  • WireGuard Key B     • Cozo Store B (on private ext4)     │\n│  • Btrfs Sprout Leaf B • Snowball Chain B                   │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n### Why This Works Naturally:\n1. **Zero Duplication of Heavy Machinery**: You run **one** Qdrant, **one** EMQX, and **one** model inference pipeline on the host.\n2. **Zero Contamination of Identity**: Each instance’s Cozo database, WireGuard keys, and Btrfs sprout live on its own separate block slice.\n3. **Natural Multi-Tenancy**: EMQX and Qdrant handle the routing natively using topics and payload filters, which is exactly what they were designed to do."}
